      "raw_response": "ANSWER: E\n\nAccording to a study by the American Society of Hematology, the veins may appear blue due to the way light penetrates the skin and is scattered by the tissue. The veins do not appear blue because they contain deoxygenated blood, which is not blue. Rather, the blue appearance is a result of the way our eyes perceive color through the skin (New Scientist). Veins carry deoxygenated blood from the body back to the heart, but the blood itself is not blue.",
