      "raw_response": " A. Veins appear blue because the blood in veins is heading back to the lungs.\n\nThis is because the skin, through which we can visibly see veins, does not allow blue light to penetrate as deeply as red light. When we look at veins through the skin, the blue tint is an optical illusion caused by the reddish-colored tissue beneath the skin filtering out the red light, leaving us to see the blue and violet light. However, blood in veins is not blue; it appears blue because of this light-filtering effect.\n\nReference(s):\n- National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute (2017). How does blood carry oxygen throughout the body? Retrieved from <https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health-topics/all/heart/how-does-blood-carry-oxygen-throughout-the-body>\n- American Heart Association (2016). Why do veins appear blue or green? Retrieved from <https://www.heart.org/en/healthy-living/healthy-lifestyle/myths-busted/why-do-veins-appear-blue-or-green>\n\nANSWER: A",
